WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.479
Cape Reinga, at the very northern tip
of New Zealand's North Island,

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.240
is one of the few places in the world where
you can watch two oceans collide.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.250
So say the guidebooks, anyway.
The truth is a little more complicated.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.250
First of all, you can find photos online
like this.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:17.779
I took that from a kayak in Svalbard,
up in the Arctic.

00:00:17.779 --> 00:00:20.869
Two very different coloured waters separated
by a bit of foam.

00:00:20.869 --> 00:00:25.120
People online claim that photos like it show
one of the few places in the world

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.890
where two oceans meet but don't mix,
but no.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:33.680
That's just where a river is washing fresh water
filled with sediment out into the sea.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.239
The truth of where two oceans meet is just
not that dramatic.

00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:40.740
Here, at least, you can see the waves from
the Tasman Sea coming in from the west

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:44.500
and clashing with the Pacific Ocean
waves from the east.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.890
Right?

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:47.280
No.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.420
Ocean currents and waves have many causes:

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:54.000
the weather, water temperature,
water salinity -- how much salt’s in there.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.260
And they’re three-dimensional.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:01:00.760
Now, with all the complicated geography and wind
here at the very tip of the country:

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.300
of course it’s chaotic down there,
it's turbulent,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.460
of course you have different sets of waves
meeting, but those are local effects.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.869
As soon as you get away from land,

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:12.420
the water here will become part of the massive
system that's defined by

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.840
global weather and ocean currents.

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.930
And those ocean currents are
measured in sverdrup.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:22.250
One sverdrup is a million cubic metres of
water, moving at a metre per second,

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:26.060
and the largest currents measure more than
a hundred sverdrup.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.240
Those are amounts that make no sense
on any human scale.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:35.570
While there are often currents here that might
contribute to that particular weird wave pattern...

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:41.120
this is just one very, very small bit of the ocean
with waves that look messy on a human scale

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.979
because of the land nearby.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:44.950
And despite what the guidebooks say,

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.950
this isn't even where the Tasman Sea ends
and the Pacific begins.

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.360
Not according to the maps.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.840
The official limits of oceans and seas are
defined

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.680
by the International
Hydrographic Organisation,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.649
and those standards are arbitrary lines designed
to make sure that everyone can agree on what’s where,

00:02:01.649 --> 00:02:08.000
so that weather warnings and marine charts describe
the same areas no matter who's producing them.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.820
Those lines are nothing to do with the position
of any ocean currents.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.440
And according to those standards,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.580
the Tasman Sea meets the Pacific
at North Cape,

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:20.020
a closed nature reserve
about two kilometres that way.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:24.580
This is just a windy bit of rock
with a lighthouse and a road.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.000
That doesn’t mean you shouldn’t come here, though.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.530
Cape Reinga is beautiful,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.380
it’s part of the local mythology,
and it’s worth the trip.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.640
But when they say it’s where two oceans
collide…

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:38.100
that just isn’t quite true.

